gosh seam 08: gosh's parts get a turn in each program's loop.

For:   REQ1/FWD-1, REQ1/FWD-2, REQ1/FWD-3. Forwarding has sockets to watch and timers to run,
       and the two programs each have one loop.
What:  each program calls the seam's hooks (src/seam/hooks.h): once when the session's transport
       exists, to start forwarding and give it the side channel; in each pass of its loop, for
       how long it may wait, which descriptors to watch as well, and what to do after the wait;
       and when the session ends. The server also ends it in the child that becomes the user's
       shell, which must not hold forwarding's sockets.
Where: STMClient::main_init, main, shutdown (stmclient.cc); run_server and serve
       (mosh-server.cc).
Kind:  additions only: five lines in the client, seven in the server. No refactor of either
       loop was needed (design v2, 4.3).
Needs: 03-side-channel, 05-build, 07-headless.
From:  the fork at mosh-1.4.0-pf.7, where these lines called a member, forwarder, directly.
Pin:   upstream mosh at decd9b705eb81626f694335b8d5940538beb06da; cut against the result of the patch before it.

